00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  predicting the future can be tricky  some things are clear like the cycle of  the sun or the punchline of a joke  [Music]  other things are less predictable like  who will be elected a country's next  leader  [Music]  but before every election a group of  people try to forecast the future  they're the soothsayers of politics the  pollsters  while their successes fill headlines  if they get it wrong their failures do  too if this exit poll is right andrew i  will publicly eat my hat  no matter how well a poll is conducted  there will always be uncertainty  so what's the best way to predict an  election  [Music]  elections are at the heart of modern  democracies  and one country has played a crucial  role in shaping the culture around them  from the first time an election was held  here people were trying to predict what  might happen  and they bet their bottom dollar on it  in the 1800s and 1900s election betting  was a huge market and the odds were a  good indicator of who would win  but newspapers developed a better way to  predict what might happen  they asked people  but these first polls known as straw  polls were pretty flawed they only  sampled the people who happened to be  nearby the reporter so these polls  fourth of july celebrations and meetings  of local militia members weren't  representative of the country as a whole  straw polls were basically guesswork but  by 1936 one man was keen to improve them  george gallup  in the presidential race that year he  pit his method against a major straw  pollster the literary digest  the digest used car registration lists  and telephone directories to find people  to survey so their sample was skewed  towards the wealthy  gallup's method relied on interviewing  voters at random and was more  representative of the electorate  the digest predicted a win for alf  landing while gallup's results pointed  to  roosevelt despite gallup's pole being  much smaller than the digests  gallup was right  gallup's success in 1936 laid the  groundwork for how polls are conducted  today  [Music]  today hundreds of scientific polls like  gallups are taken for any given election  so how do they work  the first critical part of polling is to  get a sample that looks like the  population that means hitting the right  quotas in terms of demographics of age  gender education social class  if 50 of a population were female for  example  50 percent of the people polled also  need to be female  when that logic is applied to things  like education and age you get a pretty  good sample  you then need to ask that sample a  question  the wording and poll questions can vary  a little bit but  on one level it's incredibly simple if  they're an election tomorrow how would  you vote  it's the same the world over  an election  having a good sample and simple question  is the key to an unbiased poll  but once they have the answers there are  more adjustments pollsters need to do  one key issue that we have with polls is  they tend to be responded to by people  who are politically engaged  pollsters use all sorts of adjustments  to try and make the people  who answer those surveys look a little  bit more like the electorate  for example if they know educated people  are more likely to vote they bump up how  much their answer is worth  so a lot of work goes into making sure a  poll hits the mark  [Music]  but they are not flawless  never has that been more evident than in  the 2016 us presidential election  most of the polls claimed hillary  clinton would win  but when the final result came in  many were shocked  donald trump won states where he had  never been ahead in any poll taken by  any polling agency  so what went wrong  the 2016 u.s presidential election is  actually a great example of the  challenges of polling and how we  interpret polling errors if you look at  the national polls in the election they  weren't actually that bad they were a  couple of points out which was pretty  much in line with historical average  you know hillary clinton actually won  the national popular votes right  right indeed  national polls said hillary clinton  would win 52.3 percent of votes cast for  either democrats or republicans  she actually won 51.1  so there was an error of 1.2 points in  favor of clinton  and that error was actually pretty small  historically polls have had a much  larger margin for error this close to an  election  what really happened in 2016 is that the  state polls were off in states like  michigan wisconsin and pennsylvania the  states that were closest and would have  given hillary clinton the electoral  votes to put her over the top polls were  much more biased than they were  nationally in the competitive states  that cost clinton the election some  polls were up to 10 points off  these errors were partly down to flawed  methodology  the inquiry into the the election polls  found that the state level polls in the  midwest weren't getting enough  non-college graduates the sort of people  that were absolutely critical to the  donald trump coalition and so that's a  really great example about why getting  the right sample and the sample that  looks like the electorate on election  day is absolutely critical for the  reliability of opinion polls  another complication was that many  voters didn't make their decision until  the final week  so polls didn't pick up on people who  decided to vote for trump late in the  game  polls become more predictive as the  election gets nearer simply because  people are making up their minds their  preference is crystallized there are  some people for whom they always know  how they're going to vote but for a good  number of voters they're making up their  mind as they go along  if only american pollsters had known  that in 1948  they were so sure that thomas dewey  would win the presidential race that  they stopped polling a few weeks before  the election  even the weather seems to be against  president truman but that can't shake  his confidence any more than do the  opinion polls which rate him no chance  the chicago tribune newspaper even  printed their front cover declaring a  dewey victory  quite a faux pas when truman won  [Music]  so if we can't expect poles to make  laser-like predictions  what can you do  well you can make a statistical model  models look back in time to examine why  people voted the way they did  and apply the logic of those outcomes to  what might happen in the future  our study of historical elections has  found that averaging bulls together and  exploring their  margins for error on average  gives people a better understanding of  the electoral horse race there are two  types of models we make one that  examines polls alone  and one based on polling and other  factors like the state of the country's  economy or a candidate's funding  statisticians call these factors  fundamentals  a fundamentals based approach to  election prediction might say economic  growth means higher support for the  incumbent  lower unemployment means higher support  for the incumbent  in doing this it's actually possible to  predict an election quite accurately  without looking at any polling at all  take the 1992 presidential race in  america the polls were all over the  place  but a fundamentals-based projection  proved much more accurate  but when polling in a country is good or  there isn't a lot of historical data on  fundamentals a model based only on the  polls works well  that's our approach to the 2022 french  presidential election  [Music]  there are two rounds of voting in this  race so making a model for it is a bit  complicated  our model has to explore uncertainty for  both rounds separately and average the  poles for both rounds separately and  then combine them in a sort of  chained step-by-step forecasting model  first we look into what the polls say we  examine every poll for every candidate  on any given day  and use a fancy statistical tool called  a spline to find an average  this is far more reliable than any  individual poll  then we analyze historical polling data  to quantify how reliable french polls  are  these dots show the margin of error in  every election poll since 1965.  an average can then be taken  for example 100 days before the election  the polls are usually about four points  off the end result  we then run simulations on different  outcomes based on how a candidate is  polling on a certain day running up to  the election  these simulations may have different  winners every time but the more times a  candidate wins then the greater the  probability that they'll win in real  life  so that gives us different simulated  french elections with different winners  and we used that set of winners to  calculate the probability that any one  candidate would win the presidency  on february 5th 2022 roughly 10 weeks  before the french presidential election  emmanuel macron made it to round two 92  times out of a hundred  marine le pen did 48 times in 100.  so the odds were that voters in round  two would be choosing between macron and  le pen  in that match-up macron won 88 of the  time  we had to run 10 million different  election simulations in order to come up  with our eventual prediction  two months before the election the  probability of a victory for macron was  around 80 percent  that might seem high but it's about the  same chance that a footballer scores a  penalty kick  [Applause]  and they often miss  it's important for readers to think  about our predictions not as final  100 predictions of an outcome but as  exploring a range of potential outcomes  for an election it's important to  remember that  unforeseen election outcomes happen  all the time  models are only as reliable as the polls  that go into them  and when elections occur during extreme  events like a pandemic or a war then  anything can happen  but as times go on and with more  historical data available  election modeling should improve  it's theoretically impossible to have a  100 accurate election forecast we would  have to get massively lucky to do that  but we're not trying to do that what  we're trying to do is use data and  statistics to get pretty close  [Music]  if you'd like to read more about the  methodology behind our election  forecasts click the link we also make  the source code for our models freely  available you can find that link in the  description below thanks for watching  and don't forget to subscribe

